WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.010
Don't be fooled.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.980
These gruesome burn
wounds are actually fake.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:11.430
Creating effects like
this for TV and movies

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:14.000
is a rigorous, time-consuming process

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.580
that involves complex
prosthetics and makeup,

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.930
some of which are actually lit on fire,

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:20.770
and a close attention to detail,

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.276
but the result is worth the wait.

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:25.556
Daenerys: Dracarys!

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:28.270
Narrator: When a director
needs a character

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.590
who's been lit on fire,
or maybe the victim

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:32.550
of a chemical spill or explosion,

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.860
they'll go to someone like Ben Bornstein,

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.590
a special makeup effects artist

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:38.810
who runs Taking Shape Studio.

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:41.820
Bornstein has done all sorts
of special makeup effects work

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.040
on movies like "The
Departed" and "The Fighter,"

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.130
and he's done several types of burn wounds

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.600
for shows like "Blindspot" and "Gotham."

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.640
Bornstein brought in stunt
performer Kachina Dechert

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.780
to undergo this transformation.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.396
Any good Hollywood makeup artist knows

00:00:55.396 --> 00:00:58.010
that the actor's comfort is crucial.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.270
Ben Bornstein: How can
we make it nice for them

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:03.810
and not as crazy and disruptive
to their performance?

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.630
Our job is to enhance their performance.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:06.730
Narrator: And that can be done

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:09.270
without sacrificing realism.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.730
The first step for
Bornstein is figuring out

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.400
what kind of burn it is

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.468
and what the damage should look like.

00:01:15.468 --> 00:01:18.400
In a hypothetical scenario
for this demonstration,

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.520
the character has escaped
a burning building,

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:21.882
but not unscathed.

00:01:21.882 --> 00:01:23.950
This is a fresh burn
wound, and the character

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.420
hasn't had a chance to
seek medical attention.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:27.880
Bornstein: There's all these
things that go in the story

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:31.260
that we have to think
about, from step A to Z.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.020
So, say, like, you got burned
by an iron on your face,

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:36.040
like a curling iron.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:38.110
You gotta think, how hot was it?

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:39.040
How long was it on?

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.760
How long did it sit in your face?

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:42.180
We gotta think about the reality of it.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.420
Would the skin bubble?

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:44.580
Would it come off with the iron?

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:45.660
Would it be a piece?

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.100
I want this to look like she
just got out of an explosion.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.860
Narrator: There are some cheap, easy ways

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.640
for Bornstein to go about this,

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.280
methods he would call "out of the kit."

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.550
For a burned arm, he can
use a burn transfer tattoo.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:01.377
Bornstein: It's kinda
like a Bazooka Joe tattoo

00:02:01.377 --> 00:02:02.643
you'd get when you're a kid,

00:02:02.643 --> 00:02:04.751
but on steroids that lasts all day.

00:02:04.751 --> 00:02:06.610
Narrator: These are
great for makeup artists.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:09.440
They stay on all day and
need minimal touch-ups.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.820
One of the toughest parts
of a makeup artist's job

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.960
is making sure the cast looks exactly

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.420
the same on every single day.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.840
This method is great for continuity.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.740
While efficient, these
tattoos are two dimensional,

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:24.410
so they work better for
background characters.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.520
Another easy and effective method:

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.980
using Glatzan bald-cap
plastic, aka Baldiez,

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:31.930
and actually lighting it on fire.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.470
There are plenty of ways

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:36.590
to make simple materials look gruesome.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:37.748
Bornstein: So, now I'm
just cutting a sheet

00:02:37.748 --> 00:02:39.748
of bald cap vinyl,

00:02:39.748 --> 00:02:42.720
and it's gonna make a
little weird gross membrane

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.100
on top of the burn that Adam's painting.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:48.260
So, we'll just glue this on,
and we'll blend with the edge

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.220
and have K-Y Jelly
inside the middle of it,

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.210
so we can see some depth and translucency

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:53.750
to give it more of an effect.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.270
Narrator: Bornstein can
and will go much bigger

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:57.950
for a scenario like this.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:01.380
After all, this is supposed
to be a hero character,

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.270
another term for the main character.

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:04.760
So Bornstein and his team made

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.700
some large prosthetic pieces.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:09.560
Before starting on them, he
needed to do some research

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.680
so he could nail down a few key things,

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.430
texture and color.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:15.780
That research can take strange forms,

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.530
like looking at pictures of burnt meat.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:20.600
Bornstein: The textures are
cool, but it's not, you know,

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:21.540
it's not there.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:22.620
Look at this, dude.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:23.600
This is what I was talking about.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:24.960
It's gorgeous.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.994
Look at that detail. Look at those colors.

00:03:26.994 --> 00:03:28.430
If you could see, all the black

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.270
are really the only raised areas here.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.830
So, we're looking at
what could be a piece,

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:35.226
or even just, like,
maybe the Baldiez stuff

00:03:35.226 --> 00:03:36.880
that we put on top of just paint.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.550
All this little fatty area

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:40.990
and, like, all the yellow,
golden yellow, I love all that.

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:43.680
And to really notice that
the edges aren't black.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.920
It's the deepest parts of it are black.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.820
Like, if you look at, this
is just black little spots.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:50.530
And then there's a ring of red,

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.360
and then there's black way above it.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:53.810
We could almost even probably use

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:55.300
some of her tones in there.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:57.230
But I love this picture for reference.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:58.290
I think it's beautiful.

00:03:58.290 --> 00:03:59.370
Narrator: You can see that influence

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:00.980
in the prosthetic pieces.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:02.710
Look at all the cracks and char

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:04.940
and the dominance of red and black.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:08.280
Like with any prostheses,
it started with a head cast.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.700
Bornstein used that cast
to create a sculpture

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:11.780
of Dechert's head.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:14.690
From there, they got a
plaster version of her head

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:16.350
and a rubber mold negative.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:19.350
That negative was used to
build the silicone pieces.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.500
His team sculpted away to
get the perfect texture.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.450
While working, they had to keep in mind

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:26.890
that this was supposed to be a fresh burn

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:28.960
as opposed to an aged scar.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:30.356
Bornstein: Like, it just happened,

00:04:30.356 --> 00:04:32.223
and she's still smoldering,
but she's put out.

00:04:32.223 --> 00:04:35.070
Narrator: Prostheses like
this are needed especially

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:37.270
if there is going to be
a lot of close-up shots

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:38.810
of a burned character.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:39.750
Bornstein: You can't fake that.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.270
You need the textures there.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:42.310
Narrator: Everything had to fit

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:43.870
and be blended in perfectly

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:45.760
and follow the right color palette

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:48.400
so it looked like a truly fresh char.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:51.300
The team also had to
build around her wardrobe.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.340
They knew what costume she would wear

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.820
and that it would be
torn up by the explosion.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:57.530
While they didn't have to make prostheses

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:00.330
for her entire body,
they did need to decide

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.120
if there would be visible wounds in areas

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:03.700
where skin was exposed.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.230
If there were open areas left blank

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:07.593
where there should be a burn,

00:05:07.593 --> 00:05:10.740
it would make the overall
performance less convincing.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:12.610
Bornstein: We'll glue this to
her, and maybe we'll fuse it

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:13.830
with an appliance or something.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:15.533
Here's the boot line. That's good.

00:05:15.533 --> 00:05:17.007
We're gonna do a number on you, Kachina.

00:05:17.007 --> 00:05:17.840
Gonna be dope.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.000
Narrator: Hair was just as much a factor.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.380
Bornstein: I want the piece
to lay flat with the edge.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.310
We'll blend the edge, then
the hair will go over it.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:26.670
So, all of this hair has to go this way.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.860
Narrator: To maximize
comfort for the star,

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.820
they saved some of the least comfortable

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:32.760
prosthetic pieces for last.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:36.070
They started early by gluing
on the big chest piece.

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.290
Blending it into the skin is key.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:41.240
This scaly piece could have
been placed on Dechert's leg,

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.270
but the team decided it
made more sense on her arm.

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:45.420
Bornstein: I love the little black.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.620
And these little edges we
should paint black too.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:48.453
I love that.

00:05:48.453 --> 00:05:50.260
Narrator: And remember
those burned-up sheets?

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:53.400
They were put on alongside
the two prosthetic pieces,

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:54.830
layered and blended in,

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:56.427
so sometimes it was hard to tell

00:05:56.427 --> 00:05:57.910
the difference between the two.

00:05:57.910 --> 00:05:59.751
They decided to rely more heavily

00:05:59.751 --> 00:06:01.909
on the burned plastics for her legs

00:06:01.909 --> 00:06:03.580
while sticking to the color palette

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:06.543
and directly painting on
yellow wherever they could.

00:06:06.543 --> 00:06:07.630
Bornstein: So, I see a big hole.

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:09.170
We're gonna paint that.

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:10.740
Narrator: Then this massive piece

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.500
went right on Dechert's stomach.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:14.600
Notice how the edges were peeled off

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.920
and the piece was blended into her skin.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:20.170
The final prosthetic piece they
attached was the face piece,

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:21.870
which only covered the left half.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:23.290
Bornstein: This was most
of the meat of the build,

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:24.760
was making this piece.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:26.520
Her ear will come through
this, and then I think

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.470
we'll put a black char to connect it.

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:30.160
Narrator: While they
could have covered the ear

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:31.890
to make it look like it fell off,

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.200
Bornstein thought it would
have been too obvious

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.660
and instead liked the
idea of keeping it on

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:37.880
but dirtying it up.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:39.690
He also had to consider that the eyebrow

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:40.920
would have been burned off,

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:42.870
so Bornstein simply painted over it

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:45.560
so it wouldn't show up
under the face piece.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:47.540
There are lots of
different final touch-ups

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:49.948
to make sure they looked
as fresh as possible.

00:06:52.941 --> 00:06:54.720
Narrator: Other materials,
like "clean dirt,"

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:56.180
added to the color palette.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:59.070
The final piece was also
the most uncomfortable:

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:00.540
contact lenses.

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:03.360
This stage required a lens technician.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.490
In the end, everything was
so well blended together,

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:09.960
it's hard to tell what's
paint and what's silicone.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:11.610
The real winner of the day?

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:14.970
Bornstein: I think it was,
honestly, the Glatzan sheets

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:16.067
that we put on her.

00:07:16.067 --> 00:07:18.020
It really did sell it.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:21.150
I wanted to mix kind of that reality

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:23.046
with, like, some superhero look

00:07:23.046 --> 00:07:24.913
where half her head and hair is missing.

00:07:24.913 --> 00:07:27.237
The fact that we decided
to take the sculpture

00:07:27.237 --> 00:07:29.579
up into the hairline.
'Cause if it was just here,

00:07:29.579 --> 00:07:30.740
it just doesn't look right.

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:32.660
So we wanted to go, like, the extra mile

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:34.220
and have it wrap around her head.

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:37.100
Narrator: Applying the
makeup to taking it all off

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:39.690
took over four hours.

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:42.090
Computers and visual effects have enhanced

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:44.690
the work of makeup and
special effects artists.

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:46.248
Bornstein: If you marry the two,

00:07:46.248 --> 00:07:48.264
we can, like, recess crazy areas

00:07:48.264 --> 00:07:50.270
of the face or body that
we can't do with makeup.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:51.670
We'd have to make a fake head of it.

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:53.330
Narrator: So, why didn't Bornstein

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:56.520
and his team just create
the burns using CGI?

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.920
Especially when, as is
the case of Two-Face

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.470
in "The Dark Night," the
minimal practical makeup

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:04.010
might just be covered in a CGI layer?

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:05.970
Because practical effects
won't just give you

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:09.250
a better movie, but also
a better performance.

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:11.670
Bornstein: We can bring
something to the table

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:13.745
that looks organic, that looks real

00:08:13.745 --> 00:08:15.270
and enhance the actor's performance.

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:16.770
If they have nothing on them,

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:18.170
maybe they won't emote as much.

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:20.513
Maybe they're laying in a hospital bed

00:08:20.513 --> 00:08:22.890
and they're looking at their hand.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:25.600
Maybe that will help
them cry more on camera.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:27.960
We're giving them tools to work
with, but most importantly,

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:29.640
we're giving the camera tools to work with

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:32.249
so they don't have to
do it in postproduction.

